\begin_layout Section*
Physical explosions next
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
We turn to 
\begin_inset CommandInset href
LatexCommand href
name "flight V88"
target "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ariane_flight_V88"
literal "false"

\end_inset

,
 aka “Flight 501”,
 pictured at the start of my post.
 The massive US$370 million “kaboom” of this first Ariane 5 was the culmination of many small issues—
as is pretty much always the case with industrial-sized accidents.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
But the fundamental problem was incompatibility between an integer and a floating point number.
 If you wish,
 a problem of typing.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Some languages like JavaScript and Perl try to do almost everything with ‘double precision’ floating point numbers that fit into 64 bits.
 Because some of those bits are used for the exponent,
 we’re left with about 53 bits (or about 16 decimal digits) of precision.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Other languages have a wealth (if that’s the right term) of different types.
 This is often a legacy from when every bit was precious.
 Take C.
 Here,
 a short integer is at least 16 bits long;
 an int is similar
\begin_inset Foot
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
Now commonly 32 bits.
\end_layout

\end_inset

;
 a long is at least 32 bits;
 a long long is at least 64 bits.
 And then we have two different flavours of each of these—
signed,
 and unsigned.
 The floats are 32 bits,
 doubles are 64 bits,
 and you may encounter 80 bit long doubles.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
V88 went bang because programmers tried to squeeze a 64-bit float into a 16 bit integer.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The earlier Ariane 4 had an inertial reference platform where the code used 16-bit signed integers to represent horizontal drift velocity.
 They retained this legacy code,
 along with 64-bit floats,
 and tried to convert a float into one of those tiny integers.
 This overflowed,
 causing the inertial reference modules to crash repeatedly,
 and full nozzle deflections of the engines.
\begin_inset Foot
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
And of course it’s more complex than this.
 The defensive redundancy of having two systems turned into a liability.
 The legacy code didn’t even need to be running at the time.
 The programmers had left some variables unprotected to ensure that the computer workload kept under the target of 80%.
 Nobody bothered to check whether the engine calculations for Ariane 4 became outdated with the far more powerful Ariane 5 engines.
 And so on.
\end_layout

\end_inset

 Eventually,
 kaboom!
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Type conversion bedevils a lot of programming,
 especially unthinking type coercion.
 But we’re not done with the numbers yet.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
Some banker myths.
 Or are they?
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset CommandInset href
LatexCommand href
name "We’ve already discovered"
target "https://drjo.substack.com/i/212226868/but"
literal "false"

\end_inset


\begin_inset script superscript

\begin_layout Plain Layout
⌘
\end_layout

\end_inset

  how in JavaScript trying to add 
\begin_inset Formula $0.1+0.2$
\end_inset

 gives 
\begin_inset Formula $0.30000000000000004$
\end_inset

,
 a powerful motivation for using fixed point numbers in circumstances where we really,
 really want 
\begin_inset Formula $0.1+0.2$
\end_inset

 to equal 
\begin_inset Formula $0.3$
\end_inset

.
 Circumstances like,
 well,
 banking.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
I’m sure you’ve heard the tale.
 A programmer who works for a bank diverts fractions of a cent (round-off ‘errors’) from multiple accounts.
 In many versions,
 the accumulated small amounts blow out to the point where he doesn’t quite know how to handle the cash,
 and gets caught.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
But did this “salami slicing attack” ever happen?
 
\begin_inset CommandInset href
LatexCommand href
name "Snopes"
target "https://www.snopes.com/fact-check/the-salami-technique/"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 says it’s a ‘legend’,
 confined to fiction like the movie 
\begin_inset CommandInset href
LatexCommand href
name "Superman III"
target "https://superman.fandom.com/wiki/Gus_Gorman"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
 
\begin_inset CommandInset href
LatexCommand href
name "The Risk Digest"
target "https://catless.ncl.ac.uk/Risks/5/58"
literal "false"

\end_inset

,
 Volume 5 Issue 58 from Sunday 15 November 1987,
 however cites the front page of the Toronto Globe and Mail of 3 November 1987:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Quotation
In another case Sgt.
 Green worked on,
 a bank branch decided on the occasion of its 10th anniversary to honor the customer who had the most active account.
 It turned out to be an employee who had accumulated $70,000 funnelling a few cents out of every account into his own.
 He said:
 
\begin_inset Quotes bld
\end_inset

Go ahead and charge me.
 I will tell the public you have been doing this for years.
\begin_inset Quotes brd
\end_inset

 It was true.
 The bank had been rounding off (customers’) accounts and putting them into sundry accounts.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
There’s no independent corroboration of this story—
but you can imagine that banks wouldn’t be that keen to give it credence.
 We may never know whether this is a 
\begin_inset CommandInset href
LatexCommand href
name "legend"
target "https://drjo.substack.com/i/161654408/myths-v-legends"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 with a foundation in fact.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Today,
 the 
\begin_inset CommandInset href
LatexCommand href
name "catch"
target "https://makemerich.co/salami-slicing-fraud-truth/"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 is that bankers use “banker’s rounding” or round up in the customer’s favour,
 so there should be nothing to skim.
\begin_inset Foot
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
If you want to explore rounding,
 
\begin_inset CommandInset href
LatexCommand href
name "here’s a decent overview"
target "https://www.moderntreasury.com/journal/floats-dont-work-for-storing-cents"
literal "false"

\end_inset

,
 even if they want to sell you something.
\end_layout

\end_inset

 Forensic accountancy is also a constraining factor—
even if you’ve stolen something,
 you need to make sure that you can launder it,
 pay taxes (or not) and so on.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
But the fact remains—
sometimes it’s wise to use floats,
 and sometimes it’s wise to use fixed-point calculations.
 So if we use appropriate numbers and conservative calculations,
 we’re good,
 right?
 Not so fast.
\end_layout

