\begin_layout Section*
Detonating Crypto
\end_layout

\begin_layout Quotation
On November 3,
 2025,
 
\begin_inset CommandInset href
LatexCommand href
name "Check Point Research’s blockchain monitoring systems"
target "https://research.checkpoint.com/2025/how-an-attacker-drained-128m-from-balancer-through-rounding-error-exploitation/"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 detected a sophisticated exploit targeting Balancer V2’s ComposableStablePool contracts.
 The attacker exploited arithmetic precision loss in pool invariant calculations to drain $128.64 million across six blockchain networks in under 30 minutes.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Kaboom!
 It would seem that despite dealing with eye-wateringly large amounts of money,
 crypto is not run by traditional-style bankers.
 That conclusion may however be a bit unfair,
 so let’s explore.
 We’ll start at the beginning.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Ethereum is currently second only to bitcoin in market cap.
 Technically it’s a decentralised 
\begin_inset CommandInset href
LatexCommand href
name "blockchain"
target "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blockchain"
literal "false"

\end_inset


\begin_inset Foot
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
A blockchain is a distributed ledger that has multiple copies and built-in mechanisms for non-repudiation.
\end_layout

\end_inset

 with smart contracts:
 automatic,
 computerised transactions are used to decrease the need for trusted intermediaries,
 smooth over costs,
 and prevent fraud.
 In theory.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset CommandInset href
LatexCommand href
name "ERC20"
target "https://www.gate.com/crypto-wiki/article/what-is-erc20-a-complete-guide-to-ethereum-s-most-widely-used-token-standard-20260131"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 is Ethereum’s most widely used token standard.
 It was developed in 2015 as a fundamental building block for smart blockchain contracts.
 It’s technical.
 It specifies a fixed set of functions for handling crypto transfers,
 balance verification,
 permissions,
 and approval of transactions.
 A Balancer Pool Token (BPT) is a special,
 ERC-20 compliant representation of a user’s share of ownership in a ‘Balancer’ pool.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
And what,
 you might ask,
 is a 
\begin_inset CommandInset href
LatexCommand href
name "Balancer"
target "https://baltex.io/dapps/balancer"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 pool?
 It’s a protocol that fits under the general heading of AMMs—
automated market makers.
 Continuing our interrogation,
 an AMM is a protocol that continuously prices several digital assets.
 No traditional order books are needed,
 as they are replaced by ‘liquidity pools’.
 As trades happen and prices change,
 the token balances follow.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The idea is that you put your tokens into the pool (you’re now a “liquidity provider”) and as trading proceeds,
 you earn a commensurate proportion of the trading fees.
 All is fair and balanced.
 A 
\begin_inset CommandInset href
LatexCommand href
name "sophisticated algorithm"
target "https://medium.com/@0xmirai77/curve-v1-the-stableswap-invariant-f87ad7641aa0"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 is used to maintain price stability between similar assets.
 There should be no chance of 
\begin_inset CommandInset href
LatexCommand href
name "arbitrage"
target "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Arbitrage"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 within the algorithm itself—
taking advantage of different prices in such a way that however things turn out,
 you never lose.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
But then along comes our attacker.
 He (or she) worked out that in very specific circumstances,
 
\begin_inset CommandInset href
LatexCommand href
name "rounding errors in the integer arithmetic"
target "https://www.openzeppelin.com/news/understanding-the-balancer-v2-exploit"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 can cause up to 10% precision loss per operation.
 By carefully winding the wheel around and around,
 repeating similar error-prone trades,
 our attacker uses the rounding error to progressively suppress BPT prices.
 They then set up an arbitrage,
 extract their money,
 and leave—
over a hundred million dollars richer.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
You might think that the above attack is very specific,
 very peculiar,
 and very unusual.
 Unfortunately,
 there’s a basic wrong assumption here.
 Things are worse.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
The Wrath of Kahan
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Ninety-three-year-old 
\begin_inset CommandInset href
LatexCommand href
name "William Kahan"
target "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/William_Kahan"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 is the Canadian mathematician who brought order to floating point calculations by building the current floating point standard (IEEE 754),
 pretty much single-handedly.
 He has spent much of the rest of his life pointing out 
\begin_inset CommandInset href
LatexCommand href
name "how things can go pear-shaped"
target "http://people.eecs.berkeley.edu/~wkahan/Mindless.pdf"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
 Especially if our behaviour is mindless.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Generally,
 it's very wrong to believe that the real numbers 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbb{R}$
\end_inset

 can be represented adequately on computers.
 They can’t.
 Consider 
\begin_inset CommandInset href
LatexCommand href
name "the following equation"
target "https://people.eecs.berkeley.edu/~wkahan/CS279/MullerEx.pdf"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 initially pointed out by Jean-Michel Muller:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Note Note
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
[formula]
\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
u_{i}=111-\frac{1130}{u_{i-1}}+\frac{3000}{u_{i-1}u_{i-2}}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Can you see the similarity to our playing with Fibonacci?
 Let’s try some coding with floating point numbers:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

%
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

% Dogwagger newTarget=`code/wrath.whole' startComment=`==' noWarn=`yes' 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

{
\backslash
footnotesize
\backslash
begin{verbatim}
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

wrath _over_ JOIN :2 (2~ -4~) SEQ (111~) 35
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

arity :1 <wrath> 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

setverb <wrath> 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

< ADD DIV MUL OF :-1 OF :-2 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

          3000~ 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

      SUB DIV OF :-1 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

              1130~ 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

  { RETURN | } CASE NULL OF :-1 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

  { RETURN | } CASE NULL OF :-2
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

 >
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout


\backslash
end{verbatim}}
\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
You can find 
\begin_inset CommandInset href
LatexCommand href
name "dozens of attempts in various languages"
target "https://rosettacode.org/wiki/Pathological_floating_point_problems"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 at Rosetta Code.
 Here’s what we get with 64-bit floats:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Quotation

\size footnotesize
(2~ -4~ 18.5~ 9.378378378378379~ 7.801152737752169~ 7.154414480975333~ 6.806784736924811~ 6.592632768721792~ 6.449465934053933~ 6.348452060746624~ 6.274438662728116~ 6.218696768582163~ 6.17585385581539~ 6.142627170481006~ 6.120248704570159~ 6.166086559598099~ 7.235021165534931~ 22.062078463525793~ 78.57557488787224~ 98.34950312216536~ 99.8985692661829~ 99.99387098890278~ 99.99963038728635~ 99.99997773067949~ 99.99999865921669~ 99.99999991932181~ 99.99999999514776~ 99.99999999970828~ 99.99999999998246~ 99.99999999999893~ 99.9
9999999999993~ 99.99999999999999~ 100~ 100~ 100~ 100~ 100~)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
This is very unwell.
 The exact sequence (in 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbb{R}$
\end_inset

) converges to the value 6.
 But with progressive cumulation of small errors after about 15 iterations,
 there’s a false convergence to 100.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Now if I try the same thing with 24 digit precision (the default for fixed point numbers in Zyzyxia),
 then the rot only starts happening after about 23 iterations.
 And so it goes—
I need over 100 digit precision for 100 iterations.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
Fractions
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Interestingly enough,
 we can do better if we stick to fractions.
 Here’s 
\begin_inset CommandInset href
LatexCommand href
name "a neat Python example"
target "https://rosettacode.org/wiki/Pathological_floating_point_problems#Python"
literal "false"

\end_inset

:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

%
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

% Dogwagger newTarget=`code/muller_seq.py' startComment=`#' noWarn=`yes' 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

{
\backslash
footnotesize
\backslash
begin{verbatim}
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

from fractions import Fraction
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

def muller_seq(n:int) -> float:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

    seq = [Fraction(0),
 Fraction(2),
 Fraction(-4)]
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

    for i in range(3,
 n+1):
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

        next_value = (111 - 1130/seq[i-1]
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

            + 3000/(seq[i-1]*seq[i-2]))
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

        seq.append(next_value)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

    return float(seq[n])
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

for n in [3,
 4,
 5,
 6,
 7,
 8,
 20,
 30,
 50,
 100]:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

    print(”{:4d} -> {}”.format(n,
 muller_seq(n)))
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout


\backslash
end{verbatim}}
\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The Fractions library in Python has no limits,
 as it expands the calculations to fit,
 preserving the new fraction and working out the 
\begin_inset CommandInset href
LatexCommand href
name "GCD"
target "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Greatest_common_divisor"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 at every step.
 The catch is,
 of course,
 that calculation times blow out as we wind around again and again.
 There’s always a cost,
 you see.
\begin_inset Foot
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
Julia has a Rational{T} type written as e.g.
 1//6 that uses a similar approach,
 with Rational{BigInt}.
 Many other languages like J support fractions too.
\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
At present,
 I’ve however chosen not to build fractions into Zyzyxia,
 preferring simply to allow specification of fixed point numbers to a given precision.
\begin_inset Foot
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
For example PRECISION :50 1.0 produces 1.0 with a precision of 50 decimal places.
\end_layout

\end_inset

 The catch with adding more types,
 you see,
 is that you need to maintain them.
 It’s all about balance.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
Respect
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
By knowing what we’re doing,
 having respect for the limitations of the numbers we’re using,
 and understanding the importance of typing and balance,
 we may be able to limit the carnage.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Certain design aspects can help too.
 This includes the design of the language you’re using.
 If we say in JavaScript:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Quote

\family typewriter
const fred = 1 + '2' * '3'
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\SpecialChar ldots
 then we obtain 7,
 as the 
\family typewriter
'2'
\family default
 and 
\family typewriter
'3'
\family default
 are automagically coerced to numbers;
 but if we say 
\family typewriter
(1 + '2') * '3'
\family default
,
 we obtain 36,
 because the 1 is coerced to a string.
 Now,
 if a is 
\family typewriter
'2'
\family default
,
 what is the result of evaluating the following expression?
\end_layout

\begin_layout Quote

\family typewriter
(a ++ + a)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Yep.
 The left-hand a is incremented to 3 and added to a numeric value of 2,
 to give 5;
 whereas 
\family typewriter
( '2' ++ + '2')
\family default
 forces an error.
 Don’t ask.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
I’d suggest that this sort of language design builds ‘disrespect’ into the language.
 If we play fast and loose with numerical or indeed other types,
 we’re inviting sloppy,
 ambiguous code that will simply betray us,
 sooner or later.
 Kaboom!
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Strong typing rocks.
 And we already know that w
\begin_inset CommandInset href
LatexCommand href
name "here it has been studied"
target "https://drjo.substack.com/i/219692586/4-oops"
literal "false"

\end_inset

,
\begin_inset script superscript

\begin_layout Plain Layout
⌘
\end_layout

\end_inset

  it decreases errors.
 But it's not a panacea.
 The code for the Ariane 5 
\begin_inset Quotes bld
\end_inset

Kaboom
\begin_inset Quotes brd
\end_inset

 was written in Ada—
which has a reputation as a 'strongly typed' language!
 More generally,
 we already know that 
\begin_inset CommandInset href
LatexCommand href
name "no scientific model can build a ‘true’ model of reality, even asymptotically"
target "https://drjo.substack.com/p/mostly-wrong"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
\begin_inset script superscript

\begin_layout Plain Layout
⌘
\end_layout

\end_inset

  We need to respect this limitation.
\end_layout

